<h1>Raid Guidelines</h1>

<p>All members MUST read and understand this document and
know what is expected of them on raids. If there is something here you do not
understand, ask someone to explain it to you.</p>
<br>
<br>
<p><b>Before the Raid can start</b></p>
<OL TYPE=1>
	<LI>Be on time. Try to be there ½ hour before the planned start time so the Raid Leader can start thinking about groups.<br><br>
	<LI>Upon arrival at the meeting spot:<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Disband any group you are in.<br><br>
			<LI>Turn off anonymous and roleplay.<br><br>
			<LI>Turn on LFG.<br><br>
			<LI>Type "/showspelleffects off", "/dynamiclights off", and "/serverfilter on" ... these settings will help reduce lag.<br><br>
			<LI>If your computer is prone to lag, turn off some or all of the Luclin character models before the raid.<br><br>
			<LI>Turn off any unneeded chat (ie. emotes, auction, others hits, damage shields, other players spells, bard songs, etc.) Less spam means less lag and makes it easier for you to see any instructions from the Raid Leader.<br><br>
			<LI>Do NOT go AFK unless you absolutely need to. There will be a time just before the raid starts to go to the bathroom and get a drink.<br><br>
			<LI>The Raid Leader will create appropriate channels for use on the raid. These chat channels should ONLY be used for official raid information.<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>When it is time for the Raid Leader to start forming groups he/she will announce this fact. When he/she does, all members will:<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Stop chomping food.<br><br>
			<LI>Stop glugging water and booze.<br><br>
			<LI>Stop casting spells.<br><br>
			<LI>Stop all emotes.<br><br>
			<LI>Sit and listen to the Raid Leader.<br><br>
			<LI>Stop any duels in progress.<br><br>
			<LI>Stop sending tells to the Raid Leader unless told to do so. You will be placed in the group that best fits your skills not the one your buddy is in.<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>After groups are announced:<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Group Leaders will invite members and report to Raid Leader that his/her group is formed.<br><br>
			<LI>Every effort is made to make sure everyone is in a group. If someone accidently gets left out, they need to let us know immediately. The group leaders need to be aware of folks going LD, and ensure they are invited when they can get back to the game. (The main point here is that the un-grouped person needs to say something. The Raid Leader is busy trying to coordinate about a zillion things, and once the groups are formed, they need to concentrate on the next item of raid strategy.)<br><br>
			<LI>Group Leaders will assign jobs to their group members (ie. Druid A will buff, Druid B will be primary healer, etc.) Know your primary role and for whom you are back-up.<br><br>
			<LI>Group Leaders will make sure everyone in their groups know who is the main assist and that all members have a hotkey to that effect. It does not hurt to have a hotkey for secondary assist as well.<br><br>
			<LI>When all groups are formed and roles assigned, groups will begin buffing their members. Specialty buffs such as group Magic Resist will be done for each group by whomever has the spell. (thank you Verant for /TGB)<br><br>
			<LI>When your group is fully buffed, each caster will report Mana status to their Group Leader. Group Leaders will then report tot he Raid Leader "group 1 is buffed and at 50% mana."<br><br>
			<LI>Time will be allowed to go AFK. Try to use this time to get drinks, munchies, have that cigarette you're not allowed to have inside, and respond to the call of nature. Please try to be fast and make sure your Group Leader knows that you are AFK and when you are back. If you need to go AFK for a long period of time, you are not ready for this raid and should remove yourself.<br><br>
			<LI>When all group members are at full mana Group Leaders will report to Raid Leader; "group 1 is ready to go."<br><br>
			<LI>At this point the Raid Leader will talk about any special strategies and give warnings (ie. don't sit against the left wall) and let everyone know that we are ready to go.<br><br>
			<LI>Do not send tells to the Raid Leader unless you are told to do so. If you have a question, ask it in group. If it cannot be answered there, the Group Leader will ask the proper person.<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>Raids are intended to last a good while and players should plan to be there for the duration. Naturally things come up and people have to leave, but this should be the exception on raids.<BR><BR>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>If you don't have the time to stay for the duration, let the raid leader know ahead of time or wait until the next raid.<br><br>
			<LI>Remember that everyone on the raid helped you get the drop you wanted, so it is only right that you stay on the raid to help others get what they want.<br><br>
			<LI>If you do have to leave in the middle, please be sure you tell your group and the raid leaders that you must leave.<br><br>
			<LI>Please keep in mind that we cannot ask one of the porters to take you out. He/she will not want to change their bind to the area we are fighting in and it could be quite hard for them to get back.<br><br>
			
</OL>
<br><br>
<hr size="2" noshade width=75%>
<br><br>

<p><b>Getting Started On The Raid.</b> This seems to be the hardest part for many guilds.</p>
<OL TYPE=1>
	<LI>Entering the zone or fighting area:<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Let the tanks zone in first followed closely by primary healers.<br><br>
			<LI>Once in the zone, DO NOT MOVE AROUND; stay close to the zone area until told to move by the Raid Leader.<br><br>
			<LI>If we get aggro at zone it is just the first fight of the raid, there is no difference. Hit assist, talk to your group and fight the way we planned.<br><br>
			<LI>When we are all in the zone, Group Leaders will tell the Raid Leader "Group 1 in zone and ready to go."<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>Moving to Camp:<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>When the Raid Leader says it is time to move, everyone stand up and move as a unit. The closer we are the less chance of a wrong turn or an aggro that nobody knows about. DO NOT run ahead just because you think you know where we are going, stay with pack.<br><br>
			<LI>Once we get to our camp area, sit or stand behind the main assist. He/she needs to have a clear view of any incoming mobs.<br><br>
			<LI>Once again, Group Leaders will tell the Raid Leader that their group is in place and ready to go.<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>Let the fighting begin:<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Everyone should listen to and follow the Raid Leader's guidelines. Those who can't/won't will be asked to leave the raid.<BR><BR>
			<LI>At this point we will all know who is pulling (there will only be one puller in most cases), we will know who is main assist, and we will all know what is expected from each of us.<br><br>
			<LI>If we have an enchanter, he/she will be in charge of crowd control.<br><br>
			<LI>If there is no enchanter on the raid, someone will be designated to pull and park adds.<br><br>
			<LI>Everyone else will stay put, assist the main, and let us know if you are getting beat on by adds. Running around only invites trouble. Whoever is getting hit will use the designated raid channel to announce the add. DO NOT change attack targets. Let the enchanter or designated parker render the add harmless until the main assist announces the next target.<br><br>
			<LI>After the fight, casters will report their mana status to Group Leaders and Group Leaders will report to the Raid Leader.<br><br>
			<LI>When we are ready for the next fight the Raid Leader will let the puller know.<br><br>
			<LI>DO NOT PANIC! As we work together we will get to trust the guy or gal next to us to do their job while we do ours. Keep calm, do your job, and remember that people do die on raids. If someone goes down, the Group Leader will announce the death (or group healer if Group Leader dies). When the fight is over we will do the rezzes, med up, and get on with the raid.<br><br>
			<LI>Leave the fight and zone ONLY if the Raid Leader calls to zone. He/she should do this loudly and repeatedly. If is rare that the whole raid will need to zone, and in most real raids it will cause more problems than it will solve. If things go really badly, the best thing to do is try to camp out the clerics and have the monks feign death. The monks and clerics can then coordinate getting everyone back to the raid.<br><br>
			<LI>If you are going to die on a raid, it is MUCH better that you die in the camp. In many zones it is a very bad thing to have to run all over finding corpses, and even if it is not dangerous in the raid zone it slows everything down. So please, don't panic, stay in camp, and take one for the team.<br><br>
			<LI>It is very important to try to keep the mob in one place. This is not always possible but it is always preferable. A mob that is in one spot the entire fight is at its most vulnerable; casters are not being hit and heals can come when needed. Melee know where they have to be to hit their target so no "your target is too far away" messages. Rogues can easily get behind the mob to backstab. Part of this is knowing when to nuke, snare, dot, slow, or debuff the mobs. If you are a caster and are not sure when to nuke, watch another of the same class. If still in doubt, wait til the mob is to half life then burn them to the ground. That said, it is a waste of mana to toss in a 600+ nuke when the mob has turned to run (unless the mob could not be snared and has to die before it aggros others).<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>Looting mob corpses.<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Each group will have a designated Master Looter. This is the only person in each group that will loot other than Lore Items.<br><br>
			<LI>The Master Looter will call out loot if any, "MT" if the corpses is empty, "vendor" if the loot is general junk. Everything but no drop items will be looted for later distribution.<br><br>
			<LI>No Drop items will be worked out at the time of the mobs untimely death.<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>Loot Distribution.<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>All loot (except no drop and vendor loot) will be rolled on after the raid. If you have to leave early and want a chance at a particular item, you may designate an officer or trusted friend to roll for you at the appropriate time.<BR><BR>
			<LI>The Raid Leader/Organizer will have first choice of the loot. They may pick any one item as their "prize" for putting forth the effort of organizing the event.<BR><BR>
			<LI>The Master Looter for each group will list each item (including stats and who can use) before the rolling begins. This is so everyone knows what it available and may choose which items they are interested in before the rolling begins.<BR><BR>
			<LI>Participants may roll on items usable by any of their toons (main or alternates). Please try to think of others when deciding to roll. If you don't need it even if you can use it (i.e. you have something better), please leave it for someone whom it will help.<BR><BR>
			<LI>Once a person has won an item, they cannot roll again until everyone who was on the raid has won something.<BR><BR>
			<LI>Fake rolling or spam rolling (rolling repeatedly) will disqualify you from the real rolling. (Fake/spam rolling is extremely confusing and annoys others - plus, it wastes time.)<BR><BR>
			<LI>Master Looters will sell vendor loot and the money will go to casters to pay for spell reagents (ie. peridot for symbol). Left over cash should go to the guild vault.<BR><BR>
		</OL>
			
</OL>
<br><br>
<p>As you can see, the most important parts of a successful raid are communication and organization. You must tell your Group Leader your status, the Group Leaders must let the Raid Leader know their group status, and the Raid Leader must let all members know what is going on. Stay calm, be patient, and talk to your group. Once the raid is settled in and we are working as a unit there will be plenty of time for jokes and emotes.</p>

<p>Also, keep in mind that raids are not for experience. Yes, someone will get the exp from the kill but that is not the reason for raiding. Raids are for loot, quests, and general fun. Whenever it is practical, we will try to spread the experience around, but it will not always work that way just because of the nature of groups and the game.</p>

<hr noshade size=2 width=75%>
<br><br>
<p><b>Duties of Group Leaders</b></p>
<OL TYPE=1>
	<LI>Establish contact with the Raid Leader as soon as your group is formed.<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>This includes telling him/her that group is formed or that XXX has not responded to invite or subsequent tells.<br><br>
			<LI>Find out who is main assist.<br><br>
			<LI>Ask if your group will have a particular job on the raid.<br><br>
		</OL>
	<LI>Form the group that the Raid Leader told you to form, not the one you want.<br><br>
	<LI>Talk to your group.<br><br>
		<OL TYPE=a>
			<LI>Let each person know his/her job within the group and within the raid.<br><br>
			<LI>Tell them who is main assist and make sure they have a hotkey for it. If someone does not know how to make a hotkey, explain it.<br><br>
			<LI>Let everyone know what buffs to use (ie. If a cleric is going to cast Heroic Bond on the group don't have a druid throw away 700 mana casting Protection of Nature). If we will be fighting casters make sure magic, cold and fire resistance buffs are used.<br><br>
			<LI>Make sure everyone knows what group they are in (ie. group 1, 2, 3, etc) and that you are the Group Leader, not just the person that did the invites.<br><br>
			<LI>Tell everyone that they must report mana status to you and let you know of any problems (ie. poisoned, diseased, really gotta pee, etc).<br><br>
			<LI>Talk as much as you can. Make sure you tell your group even stupid things that you are sure they already know. If they don't like it, tell them it is better safe than sorry.<br><br>
			<LI>Keep good communication with the Raid Leader. Let him/her know when your group needs a med break, needs to rebuff, has a linkdead player, or if Soandso is AFK again.<br><br>
			<LI>Let your group know that we are moving camp in X minutes and get a response from each. It slows a raid down considerably to have everyone all set up at the next camp only to see Soandso asking in guild chat "hey, where did you guys go?"<br><br>
			<LI>Make sure your group knows to get their questions answered in group and not to send tells to the Raid Leader unless asked. Good communication depends on keeping the channels as free of spam as possible.<br><br>
		</OL>
</OL>
<p>As with the rules for general members it comes down to communication. The more your group members know the better the raid will go. The more the Raid Leader knows about your group the easier it is for him/her to do their job.</p>
<hr noshade size=2 width=75%>
<br><br>
<p><b>Duties of the Raid Leader</b></p>

<OL TYPE=1>
	<LI>Your first duty as Raid Leader is to know the zone we will be raiding. That does not mean that you have to know everyone spawn point and every drop. It does mean that you should know at least a few relatively safe camp areas, how to get there safely, any special needs (ie. enduring breath), and general knowledge of the type of fighting we will be doing.<br><br>
	<LI>Next you MUST know the people you select to be Group Leaders. They are your link to the raid, the ones that will let you know when it is safe to pull the boss mob. They must be willing and able to LEAD their group and to communicate with you in a timely manner. Do not choose your Group Leaders based on friendship or last months raid, but rather on knowledge of the raid being conducted.<br><br>
	<LI>Now comes the first hard part; forming balanced groups. This is actually quite simple. Just put one high level cleric, one experienced enchanter, two very good melee toons (at least one of these a super tank), a shaman, and a druid wizard or magician in each group and everyone will be happy. Now back to the real world. you will have a list of people that are LFG, and looking at the list you will not have what you need to form X number of balanced groups. It is your job to untangle this list and make sure that each group has as much of what they need as possible. Every group needs a healer, put the clerics with the big tanks and minor healers with the secondary tanks and casters. Spread the buffs around. Make sure there is some sort of melee in each group to help save the casters. There are no hard and fast rules for this as we never know who will show up at a given raid. You just have to do your best and learn from any mistakes you make.<br><br>
	<LI>Once you have formed the groups, make sure that there is good communication between you and your Group Leaders. Also, make sure there is good communication between your Group Leaders and their respective groups.<br><br>
	<LI>The Raid Leader should make sure everyone on the raid understands proper communication. Explain that all "general members" of the raid should ask questions in their group. Group Leaders and the puller should be the only ones sending tells to the Raid Leader. Part of this is letting people know what is going on. If we are waiting for a rez or for casters to med up, let the raid know. The more information they have the happier they will be.<br><br>
	<LI>When all Group Leaders have reported that they are formed, buffed, medded and redy to go, you will have to get the attention of everyone on the raid so that you can tell them what you will be doing. Do not be afraid to say "Soandso, please sit down and stop clicking your jboots."<br><br>
	<LI>When you have their attention, you will give any special instructions, ask your Group Leaders if they are all set, and tell the groups to zone in. If there is any reason to think you will be jumped at the zone, set a zone in order (ie. group 1 is main tanks with highest cleric, group 2 is secondary melee, group 3 is casters. In this case you would perhaps say... group 1 will zone in first, followed 30 seconds later by group 3 and group 2 will stay here until we have control of the zone in area.)<br><br>
	<LI>When everyone is in the zone and control is established, you should ask your Group Leaders to let you know the status of their group.<br><br>
	<LI>Next is to get to the first camp spot. Make sure your Group Leaders know if invis is needed and that they have a general idea of where you are going.<br><br>
	<LI>Once at camp, put people where you want them (ie. casters in the left corner, melees in front of them, main tank at the very front). Make sure order is established before allowing the puller to leave the camp.<br><br>
	<LI>At this point, your biggest job will be to maintain raid discipline. Make sure people stay in camp behind the main tank and are paying attention. If you have to STOP the raid until order is restored, don't be afraid to do so.<br><br>
	<LI>It will be your job to let the puller know when to pull, let your Group Leaders know when the raid is moving to another camp, etc.<br><br>
</OL>
<p>Once again, it comes down to communication. You cannot overcommunicate with your raid. This, and a bit of zone knowledge will make any raid a success.</p>

</td></tr>
